WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.880
Speaker 1: A number of you sent me a clip
of Congresswoman Cori Bush claiming that red

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.500
states have higher rates of murder and higher
rates of gun deaths.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.520
And that is absolutely true.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.280
And I'm going to go over some of the data
with you.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.890
Now, this is relevant because the story that
the right loves to tell is how red states

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.519
are safer and red cities are safer.

00:00:22.519 --> 00:00:27.390
And blue states and blue cities run by Democrats
are just riddled with crime and they're dangerous.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.810
And that's really where all of the violence
is.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.509
Let's first listen to what Cori Bush said.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:35.530
Then we're going to look at some of this data
in 2020.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:40.960
Speaker 3: Per capita murder rates were 40%
higher in states won by Donald Trump than

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.030
those won by Joe Biden.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:50.780
Nine of the ten states with the highest gun
mortality rates, including my state of Missouri,

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.710
are red states.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.140
Mr. Wilcox.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.440
First of all, thank you for your strong, well
informed advocacy.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.559
I want to build on Mr. Natalie's question.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:06.330
Can you explain how the armed pipeline and
Republican laws.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.700
A gun laws disproportionately harm black and
brown communities?

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.770
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:15.070
Speaker 2: Ranking Member And thank you for
the question.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:19.110
Speaker 1: As as you stated, what we see pretty
clearly from examining.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.210
Speaker 2: Gun laws and looking at rates of
gun violence is states with stronger gun laws

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.290
see less gun violence.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.120
Speaker 1: Why is that?

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.120
It's because it's too.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.750
Speaker 2: Easy for guns to be illegally diverted
from legal commerce, responsible, law abiding

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.860
citizens into gun trafficking channels.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.240
What are the ways that that happens?

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.240
No.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.240
Background check.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:39.240
Gun sales.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.240
Straw purchasing.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:41.240
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:46.860
So now they go into a number of the ways in
which we could strengthen gun laws.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.299
But so is this the truth?

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:53.729
You know, anecdotally, we keep hearing frequently
now, when Republicans talk about New York

00:01:53.729 --> 00:02:01.229
City, the accurate statement that the violence
rate or the violent crime rate is higher in

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:06.340
Oklahoma City than it is in New York City,
Oklahoma City, a red city in a red state compared

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.670
to New York City, a blue city in a blue state.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.330
But this is actually a bigger trend.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.340
So let's look at a couple of different things.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:18.170
If you look at the 2020 homicide rate and
if you're looking, there's a graph here where

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:25.700
you have states with the highest homicide
rates at the top right hour and lower homicide

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.250
rates as you go down to the bottom left, you
see that the states with the highest homicide

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:33.570
rates are Mississippi, Louisiana, Alabama,
Missouri, Arkansas, South Carolina, Tennessee,

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:37.150
Maryland, Illinois, New Mexico, Georgia.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:44.980
You have this gradient wherein the homicide
rates are notably higher in red states.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:51.440
You then go to firearms mortality and you
see a very similar story.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:03:00.500
The highest death rate from firearms is Mississippi,
Louisiana, Wyoming, Missouri, Alabama, Alaska,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.170
blue state of New Mexico, followed by Arkansas.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:04.230
South Carolina.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:05.230
Tennessee.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:06.230
Montana.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.230
Oklahoma.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.230
Kentucky.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:09.230
West Virginia.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.230
Georgia.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:11.230
Idaho.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:12.230
Indiana.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:13.230
Nevada.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:14.230
Kansas.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:15.230
Finally getting to Arizona.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:19.830
Okay, So there is no doubt whatsoever that
that is the truth.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:23.370
It doesn't matter how you slice and dice the
state numbers.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.560
Now, how would the right react to this?

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.860
Well, one of the things that the right says
when presented with this information, one

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.410
of the things they say is the numbers aren't
true.

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:33.480
It's fake news.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:34.480
We can't believe it.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:35.480
It's all nonsense.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:36.480
You made that up.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:37.480
Okay.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:42.599
Well, if you can get them to acknowledge these
numbers, they will often say, well, but the

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:48.540
crimes are mostly in the cities within those
states which are blue.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.690
Now, that's not actually true.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:58.629
It is true, as a general rule that people
on the left in the United States are more

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:03.540
likely to live in urban areas and people on
the right are more likely to choose to live

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.430
rurally and suburban.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.549
It's sort of a mix depending on the states
that you're in.

00:04:07.549 --> 00:04:09.540
That is absolutely true.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:15.810
But in general, rates of crime can follow
where people are closer together.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.680
But by looking at rates, we actually account
for that.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:23.270
And there have been studies done that say,
okay, fine, you can look at these cities if

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:28.520
you want, and there are very high numbers
of crimes because of the number of people.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:36.169
But you can chart, these are very large rural
areas that cross many states on a map.

00:04:36.169 --> 00:04:41.330
And as far as rates of crimes go, they are
still very high in those rural areas.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:44.840
It's just that the absolute numbers are lower
because there are fewer people.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:51.430
But the truth is, if we want to be more serious
about this, there is no one answer to this.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:58.430
Is it true that red states on average having
more relaxed gun laws increases gun deaths?

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:00.380
Yes, it is true.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:07.110
And also it is true that if you live in Chicago,
which is a blue city and a blue state which

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:13.560
has strict gun laws, you can very easily drive
40 minutes over into Indiana or what is it

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:19.990
like 55, 60 minutes into Wisconsin, potentially
states with more relaxed gun laws.

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:23.270
So it it's everything is shades of gray.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:28.430
The gun accessibility is a factor, but it
also impacts blue states that are in proximity

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:29.740
to red states.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:31.639
Poverty is a major factor.

00:05:31.639 --> 00:05:33.900
Poverty is higher in red states.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.800
Education is a factor.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.870
Education is worse in red states.

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:43.060
Cultural attitudes towards using guns to solve
problems.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:48.319
I don't have data on what the differences
are between blue and red states on that, but

00:05:48.319 --> 00:05:49.370
it is a factor in.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:55.410
If you had states where culturally it's considered
more appropriate to fight to use physical

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:59.140
violence or to use guns to resolve interpersonal
problems, well, that's going to be a factor

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:00.660
for sure.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:05.479
Infrastructure and economic opportunity, which
we know is lower in red states.

00:06:05.479 --> 00:06:08.669
GDP per capita, lower in red states.

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:10.930
All of these elements are fact.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:16.270
And many of them actually are sort of a feedback
loop where some cause others and in turn the

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:21.120
worsening of those others comes back and worsens
the initial causes.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.069
Proximate and ultimate causes are definitely
something that needs to be considered.

00:06:26.069 --> 00:06:28.220
But the numbers really don't lie.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:35.470
It really is the case that gun mortality rates
remember, rate accounts for population, the

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:41.160
rates of gun mortality on average higher in
red states, the murder rate on average, higher

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:42.240
in red states.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:43.630
They don't like to admit it.

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:46.620
When they do, they find some other way to
blame the left.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:47.190
But this is the reality in which we live

